\lessonlabel{7}
\section{Sequential Circuits}

\begin{note}
\textbf{Learning outcomes.} By the end of this lesson you should be able to:
\begin{itemize}
  \item describe the structure and operation of a \term{latch};
  \item explain how a \term{flip‑flop} stores a binary value on a clock edge;
  \item distinguish between level‑triggered and edge‑triggered devices;
  \item interpret a simple clock signal diagram and relate it to sequential circuit timing.
\end{itemize}
\end{note}

\subsection{Latches}

A \term{latch} is a basic memory element that is transparent while an enable signal is active. The most common latch is the SR (set‑reset) latch built from two cross‑coupled NOR gates.

When the enable (often called \texttt{EN}) is high, the outputs follow the inputs: applying a high on the \texttt{S} input forces the output $Q$ to 1 (set), while a high on the \texttt{R} input forces $Q$ to 0 (reset). When \texttt{EN}=0, the latch holds its last state, because the feedback loop keeps the outputs stable.

\begin{example}
A SR latch has \texttt{S}=1, \texttt{R}=0, and \texttt{EN}=1 for 10 ns. The output $Q$ becomes 1. After that, \texttt{EN} goes to 0 while \texttt{S}=0, \texttt{R}=0. The latch now stores the value 1 even though the inputs are inactive.
\end{example}

\subsection{Flip‑flops}

A \term{flip‑flop} is a clocked version of a latch. It changes state only on a specific clock edge (rising or falling). The D (data) flip‑flop is the simplest: on the active clock edge, the input $D$ is sampled and copied to the output $Q$.

Because the change occurs only at the clock edge, flip‑flops avoid the ambiguity of level‑triggered latches and can be chained to form reliable registers and counters.

\begin{example}
A D flip‑flop receives $D=0$ while the clock is low. When the clock makes a rising edge, $Q$ becomes 0. If $D$ later changes to 1 while the clock stays high, $Q$ remains 0 until the next rising edge, when it will become 1.
\end{example}

\subsection{Clock signals}

A \term{clock signal} is a periodic square‑wave that synchronises all edge‑triggered devices in a digital system. The high portion is called the \textit{clock high} (or \textit{logic 1}) and the low portion is the \textit{clock low} (or \textit{logic 0}). The duration of one full cycle is the \textit{clock period} $T$, and the frequency is $f = 1/T$.

Designers must ensure that the clock period is long enough for all combinational logic between flip‑flops to settle (the \term{setup time}) and that the output of a flip‑flop remains stable for a short time after the clock edge (the \term{hold time}).

\begin{example}
A circuit uses a 50 MHz clock ($T = 20\,$ns). The combinational logic between two flip‑flops has a propagation delay of 8 ns. Because 8 ns $<$ 20 ns, the data will settle before the next rising edge, satisfying the setup requirement.
\end{example}

\begin{keytest}
\textbf{Key test.} Identify a circuit element that stores a bit only while a control signal is high. Then name the element that stores a bit only on a clock edge. Finally, explain why the clock period must exceed the longest combinational delay in the circuit.
\end{keytest}

\subsection{Exercises}

\begin{enumerate}
  \item Draw the truth table of an SR latch with enable and explain the illegal input condition.
  \item Given a D flip‑flop with $t_{setup}=2\,$ns and $t_{hold}=1\,$ns, determine the minimum clock period if the combinational path delay is 5 ns.
  \item Sketch a clock waveform of 5 cycles with a period of 10 ns and label the rising and falling edges.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Key terms}

\begin{tabular}{@{}p{3.6cm}p{10.4cm}@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Term} & \textbf{Meaning} \\
\midrule
Latch & Level‑triggered memory element that is transparent while enabled. \\
SR latch & Latch with set and reset inputs; stores a bit using cross‑coupled gates. \\
Flip‑flop & Edge‑triggered memory element that changes state only on a clock edge. \\
D flip‑flop & Flip‑flop that copies its data input to the output on the active clock edge. \\
Clock signal & Periodic square‑wave used to synchronise sequential circuits. \\
Setup time & Minimum time the data must be stable before a clock edge. \\
Hold time & Minimum time the data must remain stable after a clock edge. \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
